+## Implementation handoff
+
+After an actionable native decision closes, create or reuse its implementation
+ticket, then claim it before handoff. The claim is an ownership token the working
+phase session holds while it is active and releases on clean exit, so the next
+Wayfinder run resumes an unclaimed in-flight ticket automatically; a crash leaves
+an orphaned claim that only a lease-repair reclaim clears. The selected chunk
+determines the Codex handoff after `/clear`:
+
+| Selected chunk | Codex handoff after /clear |
+|---|---|
+| map/decision work | $wayfinder <map-path> |
+| implementation brainstorming | $superpowers:brainstorming <implementation-ticket-path> |
+| plan writing | $superpowers:writing-plans <implementation-ticket-path> |
+| plan execution | $superpowers:executing-plans <implementation-ticket-path> |
+
+Each downstream invocation receives the original ticket path and ID, mutates
+only that ticket, and never creates or selects a successor. Direct phase skills
+do not mutate tickets; downstream skills invoke the ticket mutation primitive
+only after their ordinary approval gate. Each generated spec or plan contains
+exactly one `## Wayfinder ticket handoff` YAML block with the canonical ticket
+path, origin ID, and legal `next-phase`; malformed or duplicate blocks are
+no-write failures. A newly discovered decision creates a linked native ticket,
+adds its canonical path to `blocked-by` through the guarded primitive, and
+removes it only after that native ticket closes.
+
+## Dissent the map
+
+An adversarial review of the map, routed through the shared `dissent-review` skill with `artifact=map`. **Advisory**: it never edits live tracker state, because that would collide with the sessions wayfinder runs concurrently.
+
+Invoke `dissent-review` with `artifact=map`, the map path (see [WAYFINDING-OPS.md](WAYFINDING-OPS.md) — `docs/superpowers/wayfinder/<effort-slug>/map.md` for the local-markdown tracker), and these raw values, which wayfinder already holds:
+
+- **destination** — the map's `## Destination` block, verbatim.
+- **chart state** — `charted` for a fresh map (from Chart step 6) or `re-chartered`.
+- **closed ticket ids** — the ids whose `status:` is `closed`.
+- **tracker bindings** — the location, claim, blocking, and frontier conventions from [WAYFINDING-OPS.md](WAYFINDING-OPS.md).
+- **review scope** — what the reviewer should read. Chart passes `whole map + all new tickets`; a Work-through resolution passes `changed region: <just-closed ticket id> plus any tickets it added or altered`. The reviewer confines its findings to this region.
+
+`dissent-review` runs one rubric-driven dissent reviewer (route integrity, plan soundness, completeness critic) over the given scope, collects your honest responses, runs the neutral adjudicator, and returns apply / skip / ask outcomes.
+
+**Advisory fold-back.** Do NOT autonomously edit tracker state. `apply` means "recommend this edit": fold it into the map through the normal resolution flow — structural fixes applied by you, decisions recorded, forks taken to the human. `skip` goes to the rejections appendix. `ask` escalates with both views.
+
+**Humanize (advisory too).** After the fold-back, invoke the `humanizer` skill on the reviewed map prose only — the map body, the notes, the ticket bodies. It never touches tracker state (the frontmatter `type` / `status` / `claimed-by` / `blocked-by`) or blocking edges.
+
+If dissent could not run, surface a loud "dissent did NOT run on `<map-path>`" notice and continue.
+
+## Closing handoff
+
+When a session ends leaving a map to return to, close the final message with the usual narration, then a blank line, then a **fenced code block** holding exactly two commands, one per line: a context-clear, then the wayfinder re-invocation on the map. The fence is the point — two adjacent lines in plain message prose reflow into one when the terminal renders markdown, while a fenced block renders verbatim and stays copy-selectable.
+
+Emit the two commands in the **current harness's** syntax:
+
+- **Claude Code** — `/clear`, then `/wayfinder <map-path>`.
+- **Codex** — `/clear`, then `$wayfinder <map-path>` (Codex invokes skills with a `$` prefix, not `/`).
+
+`<map-path>` is the map's relative path; see [WAYFINDING-OPS.md](WAYFINDING-OPS.md) for where it lives — for the local-markdown tracker, `docs/superpowers/wayfinder/<effort-slug>/map.md`. The trailer carries the **map path only**, no ticket argument: the next session picks the first frontier ticket itself ([Work through the map](#work-through-the-map), step 2). The narration above the trailer may still name the next ticket and flag HITL — that is context, not a command.
